class KadasAttachmentUtils
{
%Docstring(signature="appended")
Images kept in the project archive and referenced from rich text as
"attachment:///name" URLs.

:py:class:`QgsRichTextEditor` embeds an inserted image inline, as a
base64 data: URL at the file's original resolution.
:py:func:`~materializeInlineImages` moves those into the project archive
instead, so the stored markup stays small and the file behind an image
can still be opened at its full size.
%End

%TypeHeaderCode
#include "kadas/gui/kadasattachmentutils.h"
%End
  public:
    static bool isIdentifier( const QString &url );
%Docstring
``True`` if ``url`` references a project attachment, in any spelling
Kadas has written.
%End

    static QString canonicalIdentifier( const QString &identifier );
%Docstring
``identifier`` in the canonical "attachment:///name" spelling, the only
one :py:class:`QgsProject` resolves.
%End

    static QString resolve( const QString &identifier );
%Docstring
Absolute path ``identifier`` points at, or an empty string when no such
file exists.
%End

    static QString materializeInlineImages( const QString &html, int maxStoredSize = 1920, int maxDisplaySize = 280 );
%Docstring
Rewrites every inline data: image in ``html`` into a project attachment
and returns the adjusted markup. Images that are already attachments,
and markup with no inline images at all, are returned untouched.

The stored file is capped at ``maxStoredSize`` pixels on its longest
edge, so a phone photo cannot bloat the project, while the markup
carries a display size of at most ``maxDisplaySize`` pixels on its
longest edge — the two are deliberately different, so an image shown
small in a tooltip still opens usefully large.
%End

    static QString attachImage( const QImage &image, const QString &suffix, int maxStoredSize = 1920 );
%Docstring
Stores ``image`` in the project archive as a ``suffix`` file and returns
its attachment identifier, or an empty string if it could not be
written.

Capped at ``maxStoredSize`` pixels on its longest edge, so that a phone
photo cannot bloat the project while still being worth opening full
size.
%End

    static bool clampImageDisplaySize( QTextDocument *document, int maxDisplaySize = 280 );
%Docstring
Clamps every image in ``document`` to at most ``maxDisplaySize`` pixels
on its longest edge, preserving aspect ratio and leaving the file behind
it untouched. Returns ``True`` if anything changed.

Bounding the longest edge rather than the width is what keeps a portrait
photo inside the tooltip it will be shown in, which does not grow.

:py:class:`QgsRichTextEditor` inserts an image at its full pixel size,
so a photo arrives in the editor many times wider than the field it will
be shown in.
%End

    static void installResourceProvider( QTextDocument *document );
%Docstring
Teaches ``document`` to resolve "attachment:///name" image URLs against
the current project, which it has no way to do on its own. Inline data:
images keep working: Qt decodes those itself and never consults the
provider.

A "?w=&h=" query scales the image, the spelling Kadas 2.x stored its
display sizes in.

Decoded images are cached per document, since Qt asks for each of them
repeatedly while laying out and painting.
%End

  private:
    KadasAttachmentUtils();
};

